### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer wants to see the discipline of measurement, not just the cleverness of the fix. Starting with "I measured the p99 latency at three seconds, breaching our one-second SLA" immediately signals seniority. Starting with "I thought it was slow so I added caching" signals guessing.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class MeasureFirst {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(MeasureFirst.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long p99Before = 3000; // ms
        long sla = 1000;       // ms
        log.info(() -> "p99=" + p99Before + "ms, SLA=" + sla + "ms -> BREACH");
    }
}
```

```text
p99=3000ms, SLA=1000ms -> BREACH
```

### How do you find the real bottleneck?

In a banking backend at National Bank of India, the usual suspects are database queries (N+1 patterns, missing indexes), unnecessary serialization, lock contention, or synchronous calls that should be parallel. You profile and look at where the time actually goes, not where you assume it goes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class BottleneckProfile {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(BottleneckProfile.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Simulated profiling result
        List<String> breakdown = List.of(
            "Database queries:  2700ms (90%)",
            "Serialization:      200ms (7%)",
            "Business logic:      100ms (3%)"
        );
        breakdown.forEach(b -> log.info(b));
        log.info("Bottleneck: database queries");
    }
}
```

```text
Database queries:  2700ms (90%)
Serialization:      200ms (7%)
Business logic:      100ms (3%)
Bottleneck: database queries
```

### What is the classic N+1 query problem?

The N+1 pattern is the most common performance problem in banking backends. Loading a list and then querying inside a loop fires one query per item. For five hundred accounts, that is over five hundred round-trips.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class NPlus1Problem {
    // WRONG: N+1 queries
    public void loadStatementSlow(List<String> accountIds) {
        for (String id : accountIds) {
            // Each call is a separate DB round-trip
            Object txns = findTransactionsByAccount(id);
        }
    }

    // CORRECT: single batched query
    public void loadStatementFast(List<String> accountIds) {
        Object allTxns = findTransactionsByAccountIds(accountIds);
    }

    private Object findTransactionsByAccount(String id) { return null; }
    private Object findTransactionsByAccountIds(List<String> ids) { return null; }
}
```

### How do you fix a missing index?

If the profile shows ninety percent of time in one query, adding an index turns a full table scan into an index seek. For Priya's account statement query, indexing the account_id column makes the lookup near-instant.

```sql
-- Before: full table scan on 10M rows
SELECT * FROM transactions WHERE account_id = 'ACC-2001';

-- Fix: add index
CREATE INDEX idx_txn_account ON transactions(account_id);

-- After: index seek, sub-millisecond
SELECT * FROM transactions WHERE account_id = 'ACC-2001';
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class IndexImprovement {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(IndexImprovement.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long beforeMs = 2700;
        long afterMs = 3;
        log.info(() -> "Query time: " + beforeMs + "ms -> " + afterMs + "ms (index added)");
    }
}
```

### When is caching appropriate?

Caching is appropriate when the data tolerates slight staleness. A balance lookup for a dashboard can use a short-TTL cache, but a balance check before a debit must always read the latest value.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class BalanceCache {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private static final long TTL_MS = 5000; // 5 seconds

    // Dashboard: cache OK (staleness tolerated)
    public BigDecimal getBalanceForDisplay(String accountId) {
        return cache.getOrDefault(accountId, fetchFromDb(accountId));
    }

    // Transfer: always fresh (correctness required)
    public BigDecimal getBalanceForDebit(String accountId) {
        return fetchFromDb(accountId);
    }

    private BigDecimal fetchFromDb(String accountId) {
        return new BigDecimal("50000.00"); // simulated
    }
}
```

### How do you verify without breaking correctness?

A faster wrong answer is worse than a slow right one, especially with money. Run the optimized path against the same inputs and confirm identical outputs before deploying.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class CorrectnessCheck {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(CorrectnessCheck.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String slowResult = "[TXN-1, TXN-2, TXN-3]";
        String fastResult = "[TXN-1, TXN-2, TXN-3]";
        boolean match = slowResult.equals(fastResult);
        log.info(() -> "Correctness verified: " + match);
    }
}
```

```text
Correctness verified: true
```

### How do you present the before-and-after result?

State the improvement as a business-relevant number. "p99 dropped from 3000ms to 180ms" is proof. "It got faster" is not.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class BeforeAfter {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(BeforeAfter.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long p99Before = 3000;
        long p99After = 180;
        double improvement = (1.0 - (double) p99After / p99Before) * 100;
        log.info(() -> String.format("p99: %dms -> %dms (%.0f%% improvement)", p99Before, p99After, improvement));
    }
}
```

```text
p99: 3000ms -> 180ms (94% improvement)
```

### What should a senior engineer emphasize about trade-offs?

Always mention the trade-off honestly. If you added caching, mention the staleness window. If you batched queries, mention the memory trade-off of loading more data at once. Honesty about costs is what separates a senior answer from a junior one.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TradeOffDisclosure {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Fix: added 5s cache for balance display");
        System.out.println("Trade-off: dashboard may show balance up to 5s stale");
        System.out.println("Acceptable: display only, not used for debits");
    }
}
```
